---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MetricCard from "../components/MetricCard.astro";
import { withBase } from "../lib/nav";
import data from "../data/results.json";

const passCount = `${data.headline.splitHypothesesPass} / ${data.headline.splitHypothesesTotal}`;
const h5Ci = `[${data.h5Live.ci[0]}, ${data.h5Live.ci[1]}]`;

const positiveHypotheses = data.hypotheses.filter((h) => h.id !== "H5b");
const boundaryHypotheses = data.hypotheses.filter((h) => h.id === "H5b");

const figureStories = [
  {
    src: "fig_system_overview.png",
    alt: "PWM system overview",
    title: "The whole pipeline on one page",
    caption: "Pancakrtya loop, world model, reward, memory, sleep, and bridge in their actual file locations.",
    text: [
      "PWM is a composite system. A trainable world model (left) holds latent state, scores creative process, and consolidates during sleep. A frozen 120B LLM (right) generates surface text. The Vimarsa Bridge is the single learned channel between them.",
      "Read this diagram first if you only want to understand the architecture in one minute. Everything else on this page elaborates one box."
    ],
    next: "Next: see how the runtime call sequence flows through these boxes per stanza."
  },
  {
    src: "fig_h1_efe_vs_reinforce.png",
    alt: "H1 EFE vs REINFORCE",
    title: "H1: active inference beats REINFORCE by ~30x",
    caption: "Mean episode reward ratio (29.72x) between the EFE actor and a REINFORCE baseline in the Phase 2 gate.",
    text: [
      "Active inference is not a paperware concept here. The EFE actor's mean episode reward on the project's intrinsic creativity reward is about 30 times the REINFORCE baseline.",
      "This is the strongest internal result and the cleanest evidence that the world model is learning something a weak policy cannot. It is measured under the project's own reward, not external human judgment; that gap is what H5c-style follow-ups should close."
    ],
    next: "Next: does the three-level hierarchy help over one level?"
  },
  {
    src: "fig11_a6_ablation.png",
    alt: "A6 hierarchy ablation",
    title: "A6: three-level Trika versus one-level world model",
    caption: `Final VFE ratio per seed; advantage factor ${data.a6.advantageFactor}x. Two of three seeds are partial timeout runs (annotated).`,
    text: [
      "The three-level Trika hierarchy converges to substantially better final VFE than a one-level baseline. Across the seeds that completed, the advantage factor is roughly the order of magnitude reported here.",
      "The honest caveat: two of three seeds hit GPU contention timeouts before finishing. The direction is consistent across what completed; a clean rerun is queued as forward work."
    ],
    next: "Next: the negative-result panel that bounds what the bridge channel does."
  },
  {
    src: "fig10_h5_live_per_domain.png",
    alt: "H5b live per-domain",
    title: "H5b: bridge-bias channel under a stripped text proxy",
    caption: "Paired per-domain breakdown. Same 120B model both sides; the only difference is whether VimarsaBridgeV2 adds a logit bias.",
    text: [
      "This is the figure people misread the most. Both sides use the same 120B language model with the same prompts. Side A adds the trained logit bias from the world model's h_t. Side B uses identity logits.",
      "On English-script domains the baseline saturates the text-only proxy and the bias does not lift it. On the Kannada film domain the gap collapses to near-parity. The negative result is the boundary of this one coupling channel, not a verdict on the world model as a substrate."
    ],
    next: "Next: the latency contradiction Phase 7 resolved (and the warm vs cold confound)."
  },
  {
    src: "fig12_ttft_warm_cold.png",
    alt: "TTFT warm vs cold",
    title: "TTFT: warm-path works, aggregate run has a measurement confound",
    caption: "Warm-model measurements (ADR-001 and ADR-002 satisfied) versus aggregate live run.",
    text: [
      "Phase 7 introduced a 4B/120B cascade plus WMReasoningTrace prefill to collapse a long chain-of-thought into a few seconds. Under warm-model timing both ADRs pass.",
      "The aggregate live run still records gate_pass = false because of a separately documented cold-start confound. The site reports both numbers separately rather than collapsing them into a single 'TTFT pass'."
    ],
    next: "Next: the H1 failure chain that motivated the diagnostic discipline behind every other result."
  },
  {
    src: "fig_failure_chain.png",
    alt: "H1 failure chain",
    title: "Why static text is a hostile world-model substrate",
    caption: "The diagnostic cascade behind the passive-corpus repair: action blindness, zero-action replay, reward sparsity, decoder bypass.",
    text: [
      "PWM had to identify failure modes that interactive RL environments do not exhibit. Static text has no causal action-to-observation transition, so the policy can learn nothing or learn the wrong thing in characteristic ways.",
      "The IDL fix and surrogate actions in the Phase 2 gate are the engineering response. The H1 result above only makes sense once this cascade is acknowledged."
    ],
    next: "Next: how to reproduce all of this from the public repo."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pratyabhijna World Model"
  description="A creative AI substrate: a Dreamer-class textual world model coupled to a frozen 120B LLM through a single trained bias channel. Active research, not a finished product."
>
  <section class="hero" id="overview">
    <div class="heroCopy">
      <span class="statusPill">Active research &middot; not frozen</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">Pratyabhijna World Model</p>
      <h1>A creative AI substrate, with a frozen LLM as the voice.</h1>

      <div class="audienceTracks">
        <article>
          <h3>If you build models</h3>
          <p>
            A Dreamer-class textual world model (Trika RSSM, EFE actor, Hopfield memory, sleep) coupled
            to a frozen 120B LLM through a single trained logit-bias channel (VimarsaBridgeV2). Substrate
            and surface are measured separately.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>If you read more than you code</h3>
          <p>
            PWM gives a creative AI an inner state that learns, remembers, dreams, and is rewarded for
            its own surprise. Then it asks a large language model to put that state into words. The
            model's mind and the model's mouth are different things.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="heroActions">
        <a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/SharathSPhD/pratyabhijna-world-model">GitHub repository</a>
        <a class="button" href="#architecture">How the parts fit</a>
        <a class="button" href="#results">What we found</a>
      </div>
      <p class="sourceNote">
        Numbers on this page are read directly from JSON in <code>benchmarks/results/</code> at build time.
        Generated {new Date(data.generatedAt).toUTCString()}.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="heroPanel" aria-label="Headline status">
      <MetricCard
        kicker="Split hypothesis ledger"
        title="Internal gates"
        value={passCount}
        note="Across H1-H9 with H5 split into H5a (substrate, PASS) and H5b (surface, FAIL)."
        tone="caveat"
      />
      <MetricCard
        kicker="H1"
        title="EFE vs REINFORCE"
        value={`${data.headline.h1RewardRatio}x`}
        note="Mean episode reward ratio in the Phase 2 gate."
        tone="pass"
      />
      <MetricCard
        kicker="H7 / A6"
        title="3-level over 1-level"
        value={`${data.headline.a6Advantage}x`}
        note="Trika hierarchy advantage; two of three seeds partial."
        tone="pass"
      />
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="status">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">How to read this</p>
      <h2>The project is not frozen. Here is what is in flight.</h2>
      <p>
        Calling a research prototype done is the fastest way to oversell it. The site is honest
        about what is stable, what was revised this round, what is deprecated in spirit, and what
        is queued for the next pass.
      </p>
    </div>
    <table class="statusTable">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Area</th><th>State</th><th>What it means now</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.status.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{row.area}</td>
            <td><span class={`stateBadge ${row.state}`}>{row.state}</span></td>
            <td>{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="architecture">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
      <h2>Two systems, one bridge.</h2>
      <p>
        PWM is a composite: a trainable world model that learns latent creative state, and a frozen
        120B language model that turns that state into text. Almost every misread of the project
        comes from collapsing these two roles. The third box, the Vimarsa Bridge, is the only place
        learning crosses between them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="roleSplit">
      <div class="role wm">
        <h3>What the world model is for</h3>
        <p>{data.wmRole.summary}</p>
        <ul>
          {data.wmRole.bullets.map((b) => <li>{b}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="sources">
          Source files: {data.wmRole.sources.map((s, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && ", "}
              <code>{s}</code>
            </>
          ))}.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="role llm">
        <h3>What the frozen LLM is for</h3>
        <p>{data.llmRole.summary}</p>
        <ul>
          {data.llmRole.bullets.map((b) => <li>{b}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="sources">
          Source files: {data.llmRole.sources.map((s, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && ", "}
              <code>{s}</code>
            </>
          ))}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="archDiagram" aria-label="Runtime call sequence diagram">
      <pre>{`
World model (trainable)                                Frozen LLM (sparse calls)
============================                          ==========================
text observation
      |
      v
encoder + LoRA --> Trika RSSM (h, z) --> EFE actor
                          |
                          +--> Citta-store (Hopfield recall + episodic store)
                          |              |
                          |              +--> NREM / REM consolidation
                          |
                          +--> Camatkara reward (dF + dI_Hopfield + Empowerment)
                          |              |
                          |              +--> sphuratta event
                          |
                          +--> VimarsaBridgeV2.as_logits_processor(h_t)
                                         |
                                         v
                                add logit bias  ->  nemotron-3-super:120b stream
                                                                   |
                                                                   v
                                                          surface text (lyrics, lines)
`}</pre>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Note on the API</h3>
      <p>
        The repository exposes two API surfaces in <code>api/main.py</code>. The v1 endpoints
        (<code>POST /v1/generate</code>, <code>WS /v1/ws/generate</code>) delegate to
        <code>PancakrtyaLoopV2</code> and exercise the world model, EFE actor, Citta-store, and bridge.
        The legacy endpoints (<code>POST /generate</code>, <code>/refine</code>, <code>/batch</code>)
        are a parallel orchestration that uses only a world-model-derived prompt prefix; they are
        deprecated in spirit and slated for unification. Consumers reproducing paper claims should use the v1 path.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="philosophy">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Philosophy meets code</p>
      <h2>Sanskrit concept &rarr; textual source &rarr; module.</h2>
      <p>
        The Kashmir Saiva vocabulary is not decoration. Each term constrained a design decision:
        what state is shared, when the LLM is invoked, what counts as a recognised moment.
        The mapping below is concrete and lives in the codebase you can read.
      </p>
    </div>
    <table class="philosophyTable">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Source</th><th>Computational realisation</th><th>Module</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.philosophy.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td><span class="sanskrit">{row.sanskrit}</span></td>
            <td>{row.source}</td>
            <td>{row.primitive}</td>
            <td><code>{row.module}</code></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="results">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">What we found</p>
      <h2>Positive results first, then the boundary.</h2>
      <p>
        Nine hypotheses pass under their pre-registered protocols (Hedges' g, BCa 95 percent CI,
        50,000-permutation paired tests). Each card cites the source JSON; click through to the
        repository for full artefacts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="resultGrid">
      {positiveHypotheses.map((h) => (
        <article class={`resultCard ${h.status}`}>
          <div class="resultTop">
            <span class="resultId">{h.id}</span>
            <span class={`status ${h.status}`}>{h.status}</span>
          </div>
          <h3>{h.label}</h3>
          <p class="metric">{h.metric}</p>
          <p>{h.caveat}</p>
          <small>{h.source}</small>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    {boundaryHypotheses.map((h) => (
      <div class="boundary" id="h5b">
        <h3><span class="boundaryTag">Boundary condition</span> {h.id}: a bridge-bias logit-channel ablation</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>H5b is not a head-to-head between the world model and the LLM.</strong>
          It is an ablation of the single trained coupling channel between them. Both sides use the same
          120B model, the same prompts and the same decoding hyperparameters. The only difference is whether
          <code>VimarsaBridgeV2.as_logits_processor(h_t)</code> is applied to next-token logits, or whether
          an identity processor is used. The scorer is the stripped text-only camatkara heuristic that omits
          VFE so the unconditioned baseline is not penalised by construction.
        </p>
        <p class="metric">
          With-bridge {data.h5Live.meanPwm}, identity {data.h5Live.meanLlm}, Hedges' g {data.h5Live.hedgesG},
          BCa 95 percent CI {h5Ci}, paired permutation p {data.h5Live.pPermutation}, n = 30 across three domains.
        </p>
        <p>{data.h5Live.interpretation}</p>
        <div class="split">
          <div>
            <p>
              Read this as a boundary condition on the current coupling channel, not as a verdict on the
              world model as a substrate. The world model's contribution lives in latent process quantities
              (VFE, sphuratta firing, Hopfield retrieval coherence, empowerment) that the scorer explicitly
              removes and that the unconditioned baseline does not have. On the lower-resource Kannada
              domain, where the LLM is further from its training centre of mass, the gap collapses.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="tableCard">
            <h3>Per-domain means</h3>
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Domain</th><th>With bridge</th><th>Identity</th><th>Wins</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {data.h5Live.domainRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.domain.replaceAll("_", " ")}</td>
                    <td>{row.pwmMean}</td>
                    <td>{row.llmMean}</td>
                    <td>{row.pwmWins} / {row.llmWins}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="sourceNote">Source: <code>benchmarks/results/h5_live_ablation.json</code></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="figures">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Figures with story</p>
      <h2>Six pictures, in the order an outside reader should meet them.</h2>
      <p>
        Each figure here gets a paragraph of context, the figure itself, a one-line caption, and a
        pointer to what comes next. The aim is to make the project legible without reading the paper.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stories">
      {figureStories.map((story, i) => (
        <article class={i % 2 === 1 ? "story reversed" : "story"}>
          <div class="storyText">
            <h3>{story.title}</h3>
            {story.text.map((p) => <p>{p}</p>)}
            <p class="next"><strong>Next</strong>{story.next}</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="storyFig">
            <img src={withBase(`/figures/${story.src}`)} alt={story.alt} loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>{story.caption}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Where this should go next</p>
      <h2>Stop scoring composite systems with one scalar.</h2>
      <p>
        The TRIZ-style moves below are the project's own follow-up agenda. They follow from H5b's
        constructive reading: substrate gains and surface gains are different things and should not be
        averaged together.
      </p>
    </div>
    <table class="trizTable">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Principle</th><th>Move</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.triz.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="principle">{row.principle}</td>
            <td>{row.move}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="reproducibility">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Reproduce</p>
      <h2>Every number on this page points to a checked-in file.</h2>
      <p>
        Clone the repository, activate the project Python environment, and the gate JSONs are the
        single source of truth. The H5b live ablation additionally requires a local Ollama server
        with <code>nemotron-3-super:120b</code> and <code>nemotron-mini:4b</code> pulled.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="reproGrid">
      {data.sourceFiles.map((source) => (
        <code>{source}</code>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Known limits</h3>
      <p>
        Full 120B inference requires local model weights and checkpoint paths that are not shipped
        in this repository. The most reproducible public layer is the source code, paper, figure
        scripts, and JSON result artefacts. Treat this site as a companion to the repo, not a product.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
